# Story 10.3 ("One image, both engines"): multi-stage build following the
# same PIXI MATERIALIZATION PATTERN as the repo-root Containerfile (Story
# 7.1) -- a pixi builder stage materializes the `python-agent-platform` env
# (Story 10.2), and a minimal runtime stage copies the materialized env +
# app code, with no pixi binary in the final image. Diverges from Story
# 7.1's Containerfile in two deliberate ways:
#   1. Runtime base is UBI9-minimal, not ubuntu -- spec-python-agent-
#      platform's Constraints name Red Hat OCP as the first deployment
#      target (`restricted-v2` SCC), so this image is built for that
#      posture from the start, not retrofitted later.
#   2. The runtime stage runs as a non-root, arbitrary-UID-compatible user
#      (GID 0, `g+rwX` on exactly the two paths written at runtime --
#      `MEDIA_ROOT` and `$HOME`; see the permissions RUN near the bottom for
#      why `STATIC_ROOT` and `/app` itself are deliberately NOT among them)
#      instead of root -- OCP's `restricted-v2` assigns a RANDOM uid at
#      deploy time, so nothing here may hardcode a specific uid.
#
# BUILD CONTEXT DEVIATION FROM THE SPEC'S LITERAL VERIFICATION COMMAND:
# spec-10-3-one-image-both-engines.md's `## Verification` section names
# `docker build -f src/platform/Containerfile -t platform-test src/platform`
# (context = src/platform/). That command does not actually work: the
# builder stage's `pixi install --frozen -e python-agent-platform` reads
# the WORKSPACE-ROOT `pixi.toml`/`pixi.lock` -- the `python-agent-platform`
# feature lives in the one repo-root manifest, same as every other pixi
# feature in this factory -- and those two files are not inside
# `src/platform/`. Docker cannot COPY a file from outside the build context
# without a second `--build-context` (a BuildKit extension beyond the one
# the spec's own Boundaries & Constraints already caps at one:
# `--mount=type=secret`). The working invocation -- the one every command in
# this story's Design Notes, local verification, and CI job actually uses --
# is `docker build -f src/platform/Containerfile -t platform-test .`
# (context = REPO ROOT), exactly mirroring how the repo-root Containerfile
# (Story 7.1) already builds itself (`docker build -f Containerfile .`).
# This also means the repo-root `.dockerignore` -- not a new
# `src/platform/.dockerignore` -- governs what reaches this build; see that
# file's own "Story 10.3" section for the platform-specific additions this
# story made there instead.
#
# --platform=linux/amd64 pinned on every stage for the same reason as the
# repo-root Containerfile: the `python-agent-platform` feature's own
# `platforms = ["linux-64", "osx-arm64-min"]` (pixi.toml, `[feature.
# python-agent-platform]`) has no `linux-aarch64` entry, so an unpinned
# build on an arm64 Docker host would fail `pixi install --frozen` outright
# with no matching lock entry for the host's native arch.

# pixi.toml's own `requires-pixi` floor is `>=0.76.2` (checked live), and
# so is every other pixi pin site in the repo EXCEPT the repo-root
# Containerfile (Story 7.1), which is still on
# `ghcr.io/prefix-dev/pixi:0.76.1` -- i.e. BELOW the floor its own manifest
# declares. That is not a latent inconsistency: this file hit it as a hard
# build failure (`this project requires pixi '>=0.76.2', but you have pixi
# 0.76.1`) when it first reused that tag, which means the ROOT image's own
# builder stage cannot currently install either. Fixing that is out of this
# story's scope (Code Map marks the root Containerfile reference-only) and
# is carried as a deferred-work item; this tag is at 0.76.2, matching the
# floor. It IS registered in `pixi.toml`'s own enumerated pin-site list,
# which this story corrected in the same change -- that enumeration is
# comment-maintained and unenforced by design, so an unregistered site is
# exactly how the drift it documents recurs.
FROM --platform=linux/amd64 ghcr.io/prefix-dev/pixi:0.76.2 AS builder

# WORKDIR /app -- fixed and short, mirrors the repo-root Containerfile's own
# `/pyforge` rationale (this repo's worktree tooling has a documented
# path-length panic in pixi-build-backends past a ~173-byte workspace root;
# see that Containerfile's own comment for the full citation). `/app` is 4
# bytes.
WORKDIR /app

# Full repo checkout, not a cherry-picked subset: this builder stage needs
# three things the platform's own `pixi.toml`/`pixi.lock` reference-only
# COPY couldn't supply on its own -- (a) the `python-agent-platform` env
# itself, (b) a SECOND, build-time-only env (`pyforge-steward`) to run the
# secrets-scan gate below (see that RUN step's own comment for why this
# lives here and not in the final stage), which is a local path dependency
# on `src/shared/packages/pyforge-steward` + `pyforge-core`, and (c)
# `scripts/container-gates` itself. Copying the whole tree once, exactly
# like the repo-root Containerfile does, is simpler and less error-prone
# than cherry-picking each of those paths individually -- this is a
# BUILDER-stage-only cost (discarded from the final image; see the runtime
# stage below, which COPYs only the two specific artifacts it needs).
COPY . /app

# --frozen: install EXACTLY what pixi.lock says, matching the repo-root
# Containerfile's own convention and rationale (see that file's comment on
# `pixi install --frozen` for the full `--frozen` vs `--locked` reasoning).
RUN pixi install --frozen -e python-agent-platform

# The activation script for `python-agent-platform` -- sourced by the
# runtime entrypoint below so gunicorn/Django/langflow/dbgpt all land on
# PATH without shipping the pixi binary itself into the runtime stage.
RUN pixi shell-hook -e python-agent-platform -s bash > /app/shell-hook.sh

# ---- pip layer: Django-host production extras the pixi env doesn't cover ----
# `pip install --no-deps` into the SAME pixi-materialized interpreter (never
# a second venv) -- generated from the union of requirements/base.txt +
# requirements/production.txt (the same files `pip install -r
# production.txt` would resolve, since production.txt's own `-r base.txt`
# line is a nested include), filtered down by EXCLUDING every package line
# this pixi env ALREADY CONDA-SOURCES.
#
# THE EXCLUSION SET IS DERIVED FROM THE LOCK, NOT FROM THE SPEC'S PROSE.
# The spec's Boundaries & Constraints enumerate the intended pip set by
# name and name only `Django`/`psycopg[c]` as the MINUS list, but that
# enumeration was written against `[feature.python-agent-platform.
# dependencies]`'s NINE DECLARED deps -- not against the 395-package
# TRANSITIVE solve `pixi.lock` actually materializes. Review caught five
# more requirement lines whose packages arrive transitively (via langflow /
# dbgpt / their own chains) and were therefore being silently REPLACED by
# an older pip build, four of them outright DOWNGRADES:
#     conda (lock)        pip (requirements)   effect
#     pillow      12.3.0  Pillow==11.3.0       downgrade
#     celery       5.6.3  celery==5.5.3        downgrade (see kombu below)
#     uvicorn     0.52.3  uvicorn[standard]    downgrade (also strands the
#                         ==0.35.0             co-installed conda
#                                              `uvicorn-standard 0.52.3`)
#     gunicorn    26.0.0  gunicorn==23.0.0     downgrade
#     argon2-cffi 25.1.0  argon2-cffi==25.1.0  same version, pip build
#                                              replaces the conda build
# conda-forge python packages ship real `.dist-info`, so pip sees them as
# installed distributions and UNINSTALLS them before writing its own -- the
# replacement is silent and total. The celery one was actively incoherent:
# pip's celery 5.5.3 declares `kombu<5.6,>=5.5.2` while this env's conda
# `kombu` is 5.6.2, i.e. OUT OF RANGE -- `--no-deps` suppresses the very
# check that would have caught it, and `config/__init__.py` imports
# `celery_app` on every Django boot. Excluding celery restores the coherent
# conda pairing (celery 5.6.3 + kombu 5.6.2).
#
# So the rule this list implements is the spec's RATIONALE ("both already
# conda-sourced; pip-installing them would shadow the pinned conda
# versions"), applied to every line rather than to the two the spec happened
# to name. Regenerate it against the lock, never against this comment:
#   pixi list -e python-agent-platform            # what conda provides
#   grep -hE '^[A-Za-z]' requirements/{base,production}.txt   # what pip wants
# and exclude the intersection. `hiredis`, `whitenoise`, `python-slugify`,
# `rcssmin`, and every `django-*` line except `django-health-check` are NOT
# conda-sourced by this env (verified against the same lock) and stay in the
# pip layer at their pinned versions.
#
# ONE PIP PIN HAD TO BE RAISED, NOT JUST KEPT: `uvicorn-worker`.
# `requirements/base.txt` pins `uvicorn-worker==0.3.0`, whose
# `init_process()` calls `uvicorn.Config.setup_event_loop()` -- a method
# REMOVED in uvicorn 0.36.0. Against this env's conda `uvicorn 0.52.3` that
# is a hard boot failure, not a warning: gunicorn's worker dies with
# `AttributeError: The setup_event_loop method was replaced by
# get_loop_factory in uvicorn 0.36.0` and the master shuts down with
# "Worker failed to boot" (reproduced live in review). The previous version
# of this pip layer did not hit it only because it was ALSO pip-downgrading
# uvicorn to 0.35.0 -- i.e. one bug was accidentally masking the other, and
# fixing the shadowing exposed this. `uvicorn-worker 0.4.0` is the release
# that moved to `get_loop_factory`; its own metadata requires
# `uvicorn>=0.36.0` + `gunicorn>=21.0.0`, both satisfied by this env's
# conda 0.52.3 / 26.0.0. So `uvicorn-worker` is excluded from the
# requirements-derived list and re-added below as an explicit
# `uvicorn-worker==0.4.0` (leaving both lines in one requirements file
# would just make pip reject it as a double requirement).
# NOTE for whoever bumps `[feature.python-agent-platform]`: uvicorn and
# uvicorn-worker are now a COUPLED pair here. `config.asgi:application` is
# served through `uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker` (this file's own `CMD`), so
# a uvicorn move across another API break needs the matching worker
# release, and the failure mode is a boot loop, not an import error.
#
# The exclude regex is anchored at both ends -- `^pkg` plus a following
# NON-NAME character (`[^A-Za-z0-9._-]`, i.e. anything that cannot continue
# a PEP 503 distribution name) -- so it matches the exact package line under
# ANY pin form (`==`, `>=`, `~=`, `[extra]`) and never a same-prefixed
# sibling: `^django[^A-Za-z0-9._-]` does NOT match `django-health-check==`/
# `django-redis==`/`django-environ==`/`django-celery-beat==`/etc, and
# `^uvicorn[^A-Za-z0-9._-]` does NOT match `uvicorn-worker==`, because `-`
# IS a name character. Verified by running the exact pipeline below against
# the real requirements files: of the 24 package lines the `grep -hE
# '^[A-Za-z]'` step yields, 13 are KEPT and 11 are EXCLUDED, zero false
# matches. (`uvicorn-worker` is one of the 11 excluded -- it is re-added
# below at a raised pin, see "ONE PIP PIN HAD TO BE RAISED" above. An
# earlier revision of this comment said 14/10; that count predated adding
# `uvicorn-worker` to the exclusion alternation and was stale by one. If you
# regenerate the list, re-run the pipeline for the real numbers rather than
# trusting this line.)
# The trailing delimiter matters: the earlier `^pkg==` form would have
# silently stopped excluding a line the moment someone relaxed a pin from
# `django==5.1.11` to `django>=5.2`, reopening exactly the shadowing this
# list exists to prevent. A negated name class (rather than an explicit
# `[=<>~!\[]` list) also avoids the POSIX bracket-expression ambiguity of a
# literal `[` inside a character class.
#
# EIGHT EXTRA PACKAGES, not in requirements/base.txt or production.txt:
# `django-timezone-field`, `python-crontab`, `cron-descriptor`,
# `django-appconf`, `rjsmin`, `text-unidecode`, `fido2`, `qrcode`. (A ninth
# explicit line, `uvicorn-worker==0.4.0`, is NOT one of these -- it is a
# RAISED version of a package that IS in base.txt, excluded from the
# requirements-derived list for the reason documented above.) These are
# REQUIRED (non-`extra`-gated) transitive dependencies of packages already
# in the enumerated pip-install set -- a plain `pip install -r
# production.txt` (no `--no-deps`) would resolve them silently, but
# `--no-deps` (needed for the shadowing reason above) never does, so each
# one has to be named explicitly or Django's own app-loading fails at
# import time. Not discovered by guesswork: computed by diffing the FULL
# 74-package closure of `pip install --dry-run --ignore-installed --report=-
# -r requirements/production.txt` (pip's own resolver, asked to plan the
# install as if nothing were present) against what this image's conda env +
# this pip layer's normal set already provide -- confirmed live, exactly 5
# names were absent from both sides (the other 3 below were found first,
# iteratively, via the same method applied one `ModuleNotFoundError` at a
# time before the full-closure diff made the rest visible in one pass):
#   - `django-appconf`==1.2.0, `rjsmin`==1.2.2 (matching django-compressor's
#     own pinned requirement) -- django-compressor's deps
#     (django-compressor also needs `Django` and its OWN `rcssmin`
#     pin, both already satisfied).
#   - `text-unidecode`==1.3 -- python-slugify's dep.
#   - `fido2`==2.2.1, `qrcode`==8.2 -- django-allauth's `[mfa]`
#     extra (`requirements/base.txt` pins `django-allauth[mfa]==65.10.0`,
#     and `"allauth.mfa"` is a real `INSTALLED_APPS` entry --
#     `config/settings/base.py` -- so these are not optional here).
#   - `django-timezone-field`==7.2.2, `python-crontab`==3.3.0,
#     `cron-descriptor`==2.1.0 -- django-celery-beat's deps (its other two,
#     `celery` and `Django`, are already covered).
#
# ALL NINE ARE EXACT `==` PINS, NOT FLOORS -- changed in review, and the
# reason is this file's own uvicorn story. Every other package in this image
# comes from a fixed source: the conda side from `pixi.lock`, the
# requirements side from `==` pins in files under version control. These
# nine were the only ones resolved live from PyPI at build time, so
# rebuilding the SAME commit could install different versions and produce a
# different image -- and the file already documents (see "ONE PIP PIN HAD TO
# BE RAISED") that a wrong version here is a gunicorn boot loop, not an
# import error. A floor cannot express "known-good against conda uvicorn
# 0.52.3"; an exact pin can. The cost is real and deliberate: a security
# update to `fido2` or `qrcode` now needs a manual bump here. Regenerate the
# versions the same way they were captured -- build the image, then
# `docker run --rm --entrypoint /app/entrypoint.sh <image> python -m pip
# list --format=freeze` and read them back (note pip reports
# `cron_descriptor`, underscore, for the `cron-descriptor` distribution).
# Every package below this line was independently confirmed ALREADY present
# (conda-sourced or a transitive pull from another conda package) and is
# therefore correctly NOT re-listed here: `asgiref`, `requests`, `urllib3`,
# `certifi`, `idna`, `charset-normalizer`, `click`, `h11`, `httptools`,
# `watchfiles`, `websockets`, `uvloop`, `python-dotenv`, `PyYAML`,
# `billiard`, `kombu`, `vine`, `click-didyoumean`, `click-repl`,
# `click-plugins`, `python-dateutil`, `amqp`, `argon2-cffi-bindings`,
# `cffi`, `pycparser`, `pydantic`, `pydantic_core`, `anyio`, `starlette`,
# `annotated-types`, `annotated-doc`, `typing-inspection`,
# `typing_extensions`.
#
# `psycopg[c]` is the ONE exclusion that is not a shadowing case, and the
# distinction matters for whoever revisits this list: this env conda-sources
# `psycopg2` (2.9.12), which is a DIFFERENT DISTRIBUTION from `psycopg` v3 --
# no `psycopg` of any version is in the lock, so pip-installing `psycopg[c]`
# would not overwrite anything. Excluding it is a deliberate DRIVER
# SUBSTITUTION (psycopg 3 -> psycopg 2), mandated by the spec's own
# Boundaries & Constraints and by "the Python layer is exactly the pixi env";
# Django's `postgresql` backend fully supports both, but the image therefore
# runs a different major driver than `requirements/production.txt` pins and
# than the `test` job exercises. THE GAP, STATED HERE RATHER THAN BY
# REFERENCE: no test anywhere exercises the container's actual driver --
# `platform-ci.yml`'s `test` job installs `requirements/local.txt`, so every
# ORM assertion in the repo runs against psycopg 3, never the psycopg2 this
# image ships. Not a known defect (both drivers are fully supported by
# Django's `postgresql` backend), but untested. It is also carried as a
# deferred-work entry, which lives in this story's Tier-3 ledger and is
# therefore NOT readable from a clone of this repo -- which is why the gap
# is written out in full here instead of cited.
# THE EXCLUSION LIST ABOVE IS HAND-MAINTAINED AGAINST A 395-PACKAGE SOLVE,
# AND THAT IS THE FAILURE MODE THIS STEP GUARDS. It was derived once from
# the lock; nothing re-derives it, so a `pixi.lock` refresh that starts
# providing a package this filter KEEPS (whitenoise, python-slugify, ...)
# silently reopens the exact defect review already found here once: five
# conda builds replaced by older pip ones, including a `celery` downgrade
# whose `kombu<5.6` requirement `--no-deps` suppressed, and a `uvicorn`
# downgrade that was the only reason the image booted at all.
#
# pip announces every such replacement with `Attempting uninstall:` (conda-
# forge ships real `.dist-info`, so pip finds and removes the conda build
# rather than skipping it). Since NOTHING in this layer should ever displace
# anything -- every kept line is by definition a package the env does not
# provide -- that string appearing at all means the filter has gone stale,
# and the build stops here instead of shipping a quietly-different env.
# `set -o pipefail` is required for the `| tee`: without it the pipeline's
# status would be tee's, and a genuinely failing `pip install` would pass.
RUN bash -c "set -o pipefail \
    && source /app/shell-hook.sh \
    && grep -hE '^[A-Za-z]' /app/src/platform/requirements/base.txt /app/src/platform/requirements/production.txt \
       | grep -viE '^(django|psycopg|fastapi|django-health-check|redis|pillow|celery|uvicorn|gunicorn|argon2-cffi|uvicorn-worker)[^A-Za-z0-9._-]' \
       > /tmp/platform-pip-layer.txt \
    && printf 'django-timezone-field==7.2.2\npython-crontab==3.3.0\ncron-descriptor==2.1.0\ndjango-appconf==1.2.0\nrjsmin==1.2.2\ntext-unidecode==1.3\nfido2==2.2.1\nqrcode==8.2\nuvicorn-worker==0.4.0\n' >> /tmp/platform-pip-layer.txt \
    && python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir --no-deps -r /tmp/platform-pip-layer.txt 2>&1 | tee /tmp/platform-pip-layer.log \
    && if grep -q 'Attempting uninstall:' /tmp/platform-pip-layer.log; then \
         echo 'ERROR: the pip layer displaced a package the conda env already provides.'; \
         echo 'ERROR: the exclusion alternation above is stale -- re-derive it from pixi.lock.'; \
         grep -A2 'Attempting uninstall:' /tmp/platform-pip-layer.log; \
         exit 1; \
       fi \
    && rm /tmp/platform-pip-layer.txt /tmp/platform-pip-layer.log"

# Story 7.3 ("Credentials never enter image layers") gate, reused verbatim
# (AD-2 -- no second scanner) -- see that story's spec for the full
# rationale of the gate itself.
#
# DEVIATION FROM THE SPEC'S LITERAL "final stage" WORDING: the Boundaries &
# Constraints line says this RUN step lives in "the final stage". It runs
# in the BUILDER stage here instead, for one concrete reason: `steward`
# (the binary `scripts/container-gates secrets-scan` subprocesses into) is
# the `pyforge-steward` package's own console script, which is NOT -- and
# must not become -- a dependency of `python-agent-platform` (that env is
# scoped to exactly the three agentic engines + the Django-host deps CAP-5
# names; pulling in a whole factory station CLI to satisfy a build-time
# gate would be real scope creep on "one factory-sourced environment").
# This builder stage already has full pixi + the whole checkout (see the
# `COPY . /app` above), so it can materialize `pyforge-steward` as a
# SEPARATE, build-time-only env the same way the repo-root Containerfile
# materializes `pyforge-container` -- and, critically, this env is never
# COPY'd into the runtime stage, so it costs nothing in the shipped image.
# The safety property the spec's wording protects -- "a finding fails
# `docker build`/`podman build` itself, not a later `docker run`" -- holds
# identically here: a nonzero `RUN` in ANY stage aborts the whole build.
# SCOPE = exactly what the runtime stage ships that this build produced:
# `src/platform` (the human-authored tree its `COPY src/platform/ /app/`
# brings in, already narrowed by the same repo-root `.dockerignore` that
# governs that COPY) PLUS `/app/shell-hook.sh` (generated above by `pixi
# shell-hook` and `COPY --from=builder`'d into the runtime stage, so it is
# genuinely shipped content and not builder-only). Scanning the hook matches
# Story 7.1's own precedent, which scans `/shell-hook.sh` and
# `/entrypoint.sh` alongside its code tree -- `pixi shell-hook` renders
# activation-time environment variables into that file, which is exactly the
# shape a build-host credential would take if one ever leaked into the
# builder's environment. (Today it emits only `PATH`/`CONDA_*`/`PIXI_*`;
# the gate is what keeps that true rather than assumed.)
# NOT scanned, deliberately: the rest of the `/app` monorepo checkout (never
# copied into the runtime stage -- scanning it would just re-run Story 7.3's
# already-proven-clean gate over content this image never ships) and the
# materialized conda env under `.pixi/envs/` (third-party package payload
# from the lock, not authored here). `/app/entrypoint.sh` is generated in
# the runtime stage from a fixed `printf` literal with no build-time
# interpolation, so it carries no leak surface to scan.
RUN pixi install --frozen -e pyforge-steward
RUN pixi shell-hook -e pyforge-steward -s bash > /app/steward-shell-hook.sh
RUN bash -c "source /app/steward-shell-hook.sh \
    && python3 /app/scripts/container-gates secrets-scan /app/src/platform /app/shell-hook.sh"

FROM --platform=linux/amd64 registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi-minimal:9.6 AS runtime

# WORKDIR /app -- same absolute path as the builder stage. This is not
# cosmetic: `pixi shell-hook`'s output bakes ABSOLUTE paths into
# `/app/.pixi/envs/python-agent-platform/...` for this exact env, so the
# runtime stage must reuse the identical path or the copied hook script
# would source activation scripts that no longer exist.
WORKDIR /app

# Only the materialized env + its activation hook -- not the whole
# checkout the builder stage happens to contain (that included the full
# monorepo, the `pyforge-steward` build-time env, and pixi's own package
# cache, none of which belong in the runtime image).
COPY --from=builder /app/.pixi/envs/python-agent-platform /app/.pixi/envs/python-agent-platform
COPY --from=builder /app/shell-hook.sh /app/shell-hook.sh

# App code -- fresh from the build context (repo root), not from the
# builder stage, so this COPY (and its exclusions) is governed by the
# repo-root `.dockerignore` alone, same as everything else in this stage.
COPY src/platform/ /app/

# Same `exec -- "$@"` pattern (and the same reason) as the repo-root
# Containerfile's entrypoint: without the `--` terminator, a leading-dash
# CMD override (e.g. an operator running `<image> --help` against some
# future management command) would be parsed as bash's OWN option instead
# of being handed to `exec`.
RUN printf '#!/bin/bash\nset -e\nsource /app/shell-hook.sh\nexec -- "$@"\n' > /app/entrypoint.sh \
    && chmod +x /app/entrypoint.sh

# $HOME is a DEDICATED SUBDIRECTORY, deliberately not `/app` itself.
# An arbitrary UID needs a writable `$HOME` (gunicorn 26 opens a control
# socket at `$HOME/.gunicorn/gunicorn.ctl` on boot; other libraries want a
# cache/config dir), and the obvious way to give it one is `HOME=/app` plus
# `chmod g+rwX /app`. That was the earlier shape here, and review caught
# what it actually grants: on POSIX, permission to unlink or rename an entry
# comes from the DIRECTORY, not the file. A group-writable `/app` therefore
# lets the runtime user `rm /app/entrypoint.sh` and write its own in place,
# or `mv /app/config /app/config.old` -- the file MODES stay read-only and
# are entirely beside the point. That turns "the app cannot modify its own
# code" into a claim this image did not hold, and it is reachable by anything
# that gets code execution as the runtime user.
# Pointing `$HOME` at `/app/.home` (group-0-owned, `g+rwX`) gives every
# library the writable home it wants while `/app` stays root:root 0755 --
# traversable and readable by any UID, unlinkable by none. Created here
# rather than in the permissions RUN below because `collectstatic`/`compress`
# run in between and read `$HOME`.
RUN mkdir -p /app/.home
ENV HOME=/app/.home
ENV DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=config.settings.production

# `collectstatic` -- REQUIRED before this image can serve a single real
# page. `production.py`'s `STORAGES["staticfiles"]` is WhiteNoise's
# `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage`, which raises `ValueError: Missing
# staticfiles manifest entry` for any `{% static %}` reference (six of them
# in `platformapp/templates/base.html` alone -- extended by essentially
# every real page including the Django admin) unless the manifest already
# exists on disk. `/ht/` and the FastAPI `/api/health` seam never exercise
# `{% static %}`, so this gap shipped past every one of this story's own
# smoke tests -- caught only in review. `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`/`DJANGO_ADMIN_URL`
# have no defaults in `production.py` (`env("...")`, no `default=`), so
# `django.setup()` itself fails without them even for a build-time-only
# command that touches no real secret or database -- these two values are
# BUILD-TIME-ONLY placeholders, scoped to this single `RUN`'s environment,
# never persisted as image `ENV` (the running container gets its real
# `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY` from a runtime secret injection, unchanged by this
# story). `DATABASE_URL`/`REDIS_URL` both have real defaults in
# `base.py`/`production.py` and are never touched by either command below.
#
# THREE passes are required, in this exact order -- caught live in review
# across two iterations, neither single-pass ordering works:
#   1. `collectstatic` -- WhiteNoise's `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage`
#      (`STORAGES["staticfiles"]`) requires its manifest to already exist on
#      disk before ANY `{% static %}` reference resolves. `{% compress %}`
#      blocks in `templates/base.html` contain `{% static %}` tags
#      internally, so running `compress` first (attempted first, before this
#      fix) fails silently -- `Compressed 0 block(s) from 152 template(s)`,
#      no error, no output -- because compress can't resolve those inner
#      `{% static %}` references without a manifest that doesn't exist yet.
#   2. `compress --force` -- NOW `{% static %}` resolves (pass 1's manifest
#      exists), so this generates the actual bundled/hashed CSS+JS output
#      django-compressor's `COMPRESS_OFFLINE = True` requires (that file's
#      own comment: "required when using Whitenoise") plus its own offline
#      manifest, found by `STATICFILES_FINDERS`'s registered
#      `compressor.finders.CompressorFinder` (`config/settings/base.py`).
#   3. `collectstatic` again -- pass 1's manifest doesn't know about pass
#      2's newly-generated compressed files yet (they didn't exist during
#      pass 1); without this second pass django's `{% compress %}` tag 500s
#      at request time with `OfflineGenerationError: ... offline
#      compression enabled but key "..." is missing from offline manifest`
#      (confirmed live, the collectstatic-then-compress-only two-pass
#      attempt still 500'd the home page for exactly this reason).
#
# VENDORED-ASSET PRECONDITION, recorded here because this RUN is where it
# fails: `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage` also hard-fails on any file
# a collected asset REFERENCES but that isn't vendored alongside it. Story
# 10.1's `platformapp/static/{css,js}/vendor/bootstrap.min.*` each ended
# with a `sourceMappingURL=bootstrap.min.*.map` comment while neither `.map`
# was ever vendored, so pass 1 aborted the build with `ValueError: The file
# 'css/vendor/bootstrap.min.css.map' could not be found`. Fixed by stripping
# those two trailing comments (a pure DevTools debugging hint; the app is
# unaffected, and the tree stays fully offline/zero-CDN rather than pulling
# two more binary artifacts). IF YOU RE-VENDOR BOOTSTRAP FROM A CDN, the
# comments come back and this RUN fails again -- either strip them the same
# way or vendor the two `.map` files too.
RUN DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=build-time-collectstatic-placeholder-not-a-real-secret \
    DJANGO_ADMIN_URL=admin/ \
    bash -c "source /app/shell-hook.sh \
        && python manage.py collectstatic --noinput \
        && python manage.py compress --force \
        && python manage.py collectstatic --noinput"

# Arbitrary-UID / rootless setup (OCP `restricted-v2`): group-own by GID 0,
# rather than chmod'ing for one hardcoded UID, because `restricted-v2`
# assigns a RANDOM uid at deploy time and keeps only the declared GID.
#
# EXACTLY TWO PATHS ARE GROUP-WRITABLE, and the distinction between them and
# everything else is the whole security posture of this image:
#   - `MEDIA_ROOT` (`config/settings/base.py`: `APPS_DIR / "media"`) -- user
#     uploads; genuinely written at runtime.
#   - `$HOME` (`/app/.home`, created above) -- gunicorn's control socket and
#     any library cache/config dir.
# `STATIC_ROOT` (`BASE_DIR / "staticfiles"`, populated by `collectstatic`
# above) is group-READABLE ONLY (`g+rX`, no `w`). Nothing writes to it after
# the build -- WhiteNoise's `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage` serves it
# read-only from a manifest baked in three passes above -- and it is the one
# tree in this image whose contents are handed to every visitor's browser as
# executable JS. Leaving it writable would let anything running as the
# runtime user rewrite `/app/staticfiles/js/vendor/bootstrap.min.<hash>.js`
# in place and have WhiteNoise serve the modified bundle, with no image-layer
# change to detect it. (Consequence, stated so it isn't rediscovered as a
# bug: `manage.py collectstatic` cannot be re-run inside a running container.
# That is the intended posture for an immutable image; re-run it in a build.)
#
# `/app` ITSELF IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS LIST -- it stays root:root 0755,
# group `r-x`. An earlier revision made it group-writable to serve as
# `$HOME`; review caught that POSIX takes unlink/rename permission from the
# DIRECTORY, so that grant let the runtime user replace `/app/entrypoint.sh`
# outright. `$HOME` moved to `/app/.home` (see the `ENV HOME=` block above)
# precisely so this line no longer has to touch `/app`.
#
# This app's own `LOGGING` config (`config/settings/base.py` and
# `production.py`) uses a `console` `StreamHandler` only -- there is no
# on-disk log directory to chmod. If a future story adds a file-based log
# handler, that story must extend the writable list here.
RUN mkdir -p /app/platformapp/media \
    && chgrp -R 0 /app/platformapp/media /app/.home \
    && chmod -R g+rwX /app/platformapp/media /app/.home \
    && chgrp -R 0 /app/staticfiles \
    && chmod -R g+rX /app/staticfiles

# USER 1001:0 -- an arbitrary example non-root uid paired with GID 0, never
# read by anything in this image (nothing here branches on "is my uid
# 1001"). OCP's `restricted-v2` SCC overrides this to a RANDOM uid at
# deploy time and keeps the declared GID; `docker run --user 12345:0` in
# this story's own Verification proves the image works under a UID that
# was never baked in anywhere, which is the actual property this
# Containerfile has to deliver -- 1001 itself carries no significance
# beyond "not 0".
USER 1001:0

# Declares the port `CMD`'s gunicorn actually binds. Pure metadata (it opens
# nothing by itself), but it is what makes `docker/podman run -P` publish
# anything at all and what compose/orchestrator tooling reads to discover the
# service port -- without it this image advertises no port despite existing
# to serve one.
EXPOSE 8000

ENTRYPOINT ["/app/entrypoint.sh"]
CMD ["gunicorn", "config.asgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000", "--worker-class", "uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker"]
